---
interface Props { variation: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 }
const { variation } = Astro.props;
const labels = [
	['A–01', 'Marginalia', 'Structured essay / persistent context'],
	['A–02', 'Sunday edition', 'Newspaper rhythm / high density'],
	['A–03', 'Engineering notebook', 'Technical annotation / evidence blocks'],
	['A–04', 'Longform aperture', 'Immersive opening / cinematic transition'],
	['A–05', 'Quiet reader', 'Minimal focus / literary cadence'],
] as const;
const label = labels[variation - 1];
---

<header class="prototype-label"><span>{label[0]}</span><strong>{label[1]}</strong><small>{label[2]}</small></header>

{variation === 1 && (
	<div class="prototype-canvas article-marginalia">
		<header><span>FIELD NOTE 004</span><h3>A rulebook is not an<br />executable specification.</h3><p>What building a distributed board game taught me about state, interfaces, and ambiguity.</p></header>
		<div class="marginalia-body"><aside><b>Contents</b><span>01 / The game I imagined</span><span>02 / Before the screen</span><span>03 / Shared reality</span><em>8 min read</em></aside><article><p class="article-deck">A physical game relies on people to remember context. Software has to represent every one of those assumptions explicitly.</p><h4>Before the screen came the system</h4><p>At first, a game seemed mainly like what a player could see. Much of the consequential work lived behind that surface: sessions, commands, network boundaries, and one coherent source of truth.</p><blockquote>Rules become software only when they become state, transitions, validation, and failure cases.</blockquote></article><div class="margin-notes"><span>Margin 01</span><p>The visible interface is the last stop in a much longer chain.</p><span>Evidence</span><p>Two transports, four contributors, one authoritative server.</p></div></div>
	</div>
)}

{variation === 2 && (
	<div class="prototype-canvas article-newspaper">
		<div class="newspaper-mast"><span>ENGINEERING REVIEW</span><b>Samuele’s Field Notes</b><span>VOL. 01 — €0</span></div>
		<header><p>Systems / Reflection / 27 August 2026</p><h3>MY FIRST VIDEO GAME<br />WAS A DISTRIBUTED SYSTEM</h3><div><span>By Samuele Segrini</span><span>Illustration: shared state over a network</span><span>Reading time: 8 minutes</span></div></header>
		<div class="newspaper-body"><p>I expected my first game project to be about rules and interfaces. It taught me how much invisible work is required before several players can share the same world.</p><p>A placement becomes part of a multiplayer game only when the client expresses an intention, the server validates it, and every affected player receives a coherent new state.</p><figure><img src="/images/projects/galaxy-trucker-network.png" alt="Galaxy Trucker network" /><figcaption>Fig. 01 — The interface is only the visible edge of shared state.</figcaption></figure><blockquote>Interfaces are agreements between people working at different points in the same flow.</blockquote></div>
	</div>
)}

{variation === 3 && (
	<div class="prototype-canvas article-notebook">
		<div class="notebook-top"><span>LOG / 08</span><span>TOPIC: FAILURE SEMANTICS</span><span>STATUS: PUBLISHED</span></div>
		<div class="notebook-layout"><aside><span>00 Overview</span><span>01 The false binary</span><span>02 May have executed</span><span>03 Reconciliation</span></aside><article><p class="notebook-label">OBSERVATION 02</p><h3>A timeout does not mean<br /><mark>nothing happened.</mark></h3><p class="notebook-lead">Once a request crosses a hardware boundary, the absence of a reply is information about our knowledge—not necessarily about the device.</p><div class="notebook-callout"><span>INVARIANT</span><b>Never retry an uncertain fiscal mutation as if it were known not to have been sent.</b></div><h4>The false binary</h4><p>Success and failure are insufficient when the physical world can complete a command after the application stops hearing from it.</p></article><div class="notebook-evidence"><span>EVIDENCE / 01</span><pre>notSent · mayHaveExecuted · confirmed</pre><span>DESIGN EFFECT</span><p>The interface must expose uncertainty without manufacturing alarm.</p></div></div>
	</div>
)}

{variation === 4 && (
	<div class="prototype-canvas article-aperture">
		<header><span>ESSAY / PERSONAL HISTORY</span><h3>When Java stopped<br />being an exercise.</h3><p>A story about the first time code became a place where decisions made by several people had to meet.</p><div><b>Samuele Segrini</b><span>8 min</span><span>27.08.26</span></div></header>
		<figure><img src="/images/projects/galaxy-trucker-network.png" alt="Galaxy Trucker network interface" /><figcaption>A graphical ship becomes a game only when its decisions survive the network.</figcaption></figure>
		<article><p class="aperture-drop">Java was no longer a collection of isolated classes with an expected output. The language became the place where decisions made in different parts of the project met each other.</p><p>An interface could separate Socket from RMI, but only if both implementations agreed on the same behaviour. An object could be correct in memory and still fail when serialization crossed the network.</p></article>
	</div>
)}

{variation === 5 && (
	<div class="prototype-canvas article-reader">
		<div class="reader-progress"><span>32%</span><i></i><span>08 min</span></div>
		<article><p class="reader-category">Personal experience · Distributed systems</p><h3>Finishing and<br />being finished</h3><p class="reader-deck">Two definitions of completion can be true at once.</p><div class="reader-byline"><span>Words by Samuele Segrini</span><span>Published 27 August 2026</span></div><p class="reader-first">We reached the course submission with the required game, two transports, two interfaces, and the advanced features we had selected. Under that definition, the project was finished.</p><p>The archived source also contains a client compilation defect. Today I would not describe a build in that condition as release-ready. The useful story holds both facts without polishing either away.</p><blockquote>Accuracy is more interesting than a cleaner ending.</blockquote></article>
	</div>
)}
